# Expenses

Household spending is a list of `Expense` items (name, amount, monthly/yearly,
growth). Each item can optionally set inclusive `startYear` / `endYear`. Omit
both and the expense runs for the whole projection, which is how every saved
plan behaved before this field existed.

## Amount vs growth

The entered amount is what you spend **in the start year**. Growth compounds
only after that. If `startYear` is omitted, the first projection year is the
start (same as the old year-index growth). Income uses the same rule.

`expenseMonthlyForYear` in `household.ts` is the single source. `project.ts`
must call it; don't re-derive monthly/grown amounts in the loop.

## UI

Wizard fields: optional Start / End `YearSelect`s under Active years, on the
same desktop row as name / amount / frequency / growth (`lg:grid-cols-6`,
Active years `lg:col-span-2`). Below `lg` they still stack. Closed cards and
the plan sidebar show the window only when a bound is set
(`expenseYearsLabel`: "2028 to 2032", "2028 onward", "through 2032"). The
step total is year-1 spend, so an expense that starts later does not count
there.

## Migration

Additive. `migrateHousehold` leaves missing years alone (open-ended) and strips
non-finite `startYear` / `endYear` on live and deleted expenses. Existing
golden cases must not move.

## Growth from today

Like incomes, each expense can grow from **today** (`growthFrom: "today"`,
the "Growth starts" toggle): the amount is today's dollars and compounds from
the year of `refs.asOfDate` (the projection start with no date). Missing
means `expenseStart`: the amount is start-year dollars. `expenseMonthlyForYear`
takes today's year; the Expenses step's year-1 total passes it too. The
toggle UI is the shared `GrowthFromToggle`.

## Costs from other steps (switch)

`Household.includeOtherExpenses` (Expenses step, off by default) adds costs
entered elsewhere as expense lines every year (`lib/engine/otherExpenses.ts`):
life insurance premiums (`premium:<policy>`; term through its term,
permanent for life, none after the insured passes), homestead loan payments
(`loan:<property>`, on the same pay-down as the balance) and the Car
analysis's recommended option from the plan start (`car`, only once
`carDecision` is saved). They're fixed bills: Spending capacity never scales
them. Homestead interest is still not deducted.

## Medicare Part B premiums (switch)

`Household.includeMedicarePremiums` (Expenses step, off by default) adds each
enrolled person's Part B premium as an expense line (`medicare:<person>`),
computed inside `projectScenario` after the year's tax is priced: the tier
from MAGI two years earlier (`MEDICARE_IRMAA_LOOKBACK_YEARS`, the first two
years use the first year's), enrolled from 67 or retirement
(`paysMedicarePartB`). It must stay equal to `medicareYears` row by row,
which the test checks. Fixed bills: Spending capacity doesn't scale them.

